cartridge "DUTCHMAN'S MINE/C.M. BLAKEMORE/1988"
' LOST DUTCHMAN'S MINE
' Based on "Lost Dutchman's Mine" by Cleveland M. Blakemore, Ahoy! June
' 1988 (Commodore BASIC for all 40-column Commodore computers, 29 blocks
' on the disk; "(C)1988 CMB" on its title screen), for the ColecoVision
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu; this version by Claude (Anthropic).
'
' A text adventure: from a deserted gas station in the desert, travel by
' GOZER to an adobe house, get past the evil spirit, the timber wolf and
' the flooded burial ground, and bring the glowing diamond, the red ruby
' and the Dutchman's nugget back to the glass shelf.
'
' Screen: a picture of the place on rows 1-7 (the top screen third as a
' bitmap), the prospector, the spirit and the wolf as sprites, the place's
' name in gold on row 8, the room text on rows 9-15 (paged with MORE when
' a place is entered, cut to fit when shown again), a divider on row 16,
' the answers on rows 17-19, icons of what you carry on rows 20-21, a hint
' on row 22.
'
' Controls (either joystick): push the stick north, south, east or west to
' walk (a fresh push a step). Left button: the command list (LOOK,
' INVENTORY, UP, DOWN, EXAMINE, TAKE, DROP, LIGHT, DIG, SHOOT, TURN, BLOW,
' WHISTLE, SAY, GOZER, QUIT): the stick chooses, left button takes it,
' right button closes the list. A command that needs a thing then lists
' the things here and carried and what is to be seen here. A button turns
' a MORE page. Keypad not used.
'
' The listing's rules are kept: its ten places and exits (DATA 1700-1780;
' the gas station has none: only GOZER leaves it), where every thing lies
' (DATA 1920), every answer and the order of its checks (lines 370-1520),
' and its turn counter (COUNT, reset on entering a place): the spirit
' scares you back from the granite tunnel and sucks your breath out after
' five turns in front of the mine (angry after three) until you blow the
' bottle at it; the wolf kills you on your second turn on the staircase
' unless you shoot it (revolver and bullets); the burial ground is under
' water until the iron door's wheel is turned, which floods the river bed
' (one turn to get out); the crack lets you through carrying one thing at
' most; without a burning torch the dark places kill you after two turns,
' each turn with an even chance (RND(0) > .5); dropping the diamond, the
' ruby and the nugget at the gas station wins.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - The stick and command lists instead of typed words: N/NORTH, TAKE/GET,
'   DROP/PUT, LOAD/INVEN/I, SHOOT/KILL, TURN/ROTAT/TWIST, BLOW/WHIST,
'   LOOK/EXAMINE become one entry each; UP and DOWN are commands. A thing
'   is chosen from what is here, carried or to be seen (in the dark, only
'   what you carry), so the parser's "WHAT DOES THAT MEAN?", "I DON'T KNOW
'   THAT VERB!" and "... WHAT?!?" cannot happen. GOZER is offered to SAY.
' - DIG needs the shovel (the listing digs bare-handed; the shovel was
'   otherwise useless): "YOU HAVE NOTHING TO DIG WITH." is new.
' - WHISTLE alone away from the spirit only tweets (the listing then also
'   printed "THAT'S RIDICULOUS!" by falling through to line 1390).
' - The commands given are counted (BCD) and shown at the end.
' - QUIT asks first and goes back to the title.
' - Pictures, the prospector, the spirit and the wolf as sprites, the
'   torch's flame, icons of what you carry, a title tune and sound.
'
' House rules: tiles from code 128 (icons 128-167, divider 168, pointer
' 169, gold letters 192-255 made from the font at start-up), the tune once
' (quieter than the effects, first in the sound table), NMI off while
' drawing, `set sprite count 4` again after every `clear sprites`, nothing
' flashes faster than 3 Hz.

